---
import BootMenu from '../BootMenu.astro';
import Callout from '../Callout.astro';
---

<div
  style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 28px; padding: 40px 0 56px"
>
  <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 12px">
    <div
      style="width: 12px; height: 18px; background: var(--color-7dffa8); box-shadow: 0 0 8px var(--color-7dffa8)"
    >
    </div>
    <div class="num">A practical guide for non-engineers</div>
  </div>
  <h1 style="font-size: clamp(40px, 8.5vw, 64px); line-height: 1">
    <span class="hero">Getting going with AI</span>
  </h1>
  <p class="lede">
    From "I chat with Claude sometimes" to "Claude builds small things on my
    computer while I check its work".
  </p>
  <div
    class="card"
    style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 12px; border-color: var(--color-2a6b42)"
  >
    <div class="num" style="color: var(--color-b8ffd0)">For NZB</div>
    <p>
      There is one trick that runs through this whole guide: you get one AI to
      write the instructions, then hand those instructions to another AI that
      does the work. Once you have seen it, you will use it for everything. This
      guide points that trick at your own computer. You will not need to learn
      to code. You will need to read what the agent plans, say yes or no and
      keep your work in one folder. Go slow, keep it small and when you get
      stuck, send me a thread link and I will jump in.
    </p>
    <p>
      A lot of the thinking in here comes from Matt Shumer, who writes a
      newsletter called <a href="https://somethingbig.ai"
        >Something Big Is Happening</a
      >. He has spent years building AI companies and he writes about this stuff
      in plain English better than anyone I know. I link his articles where they
      fit. Read them.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="card" style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 10px">
    <div class="num" style="color: var(--color-b8ffd0)">Why now</div>
    <p>
      Building with AI now feels like describing what you want in plain English
      and watching it appear. Engineers have had that experience for the last
      year or so. Everyone else is about to have it too, and right now there is
      a short window where most people are still ignoring it. The other thing
      that changed is price. A result that cost a lab hundreds of thousands of
      dollars in late 2024 now comes out of a twenty dollar a month
      subscription. In AI, "this year's million-dollar breakthrough is next
      year's $20 subscription." So the thing you could not afford to build last
      year is probably a weekend project now.
    </p>
    <p style="font-size: 15px; color: var(--color-4f9a68)">
      Read: <a href="https://somethingbig.ai/something-big-is-happening"
        >Something big is happening</a
      > and <a href="https://somethingbig.ai/what-costs-millions-today"
        >What costs millions today</a
      >.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 10px">
    <div class="num">What is in here</div>
    <BootMenu />
  </div>
  <Callout title={'These tools change fast'} warning
    ><svg
      slot="icon"
      width="22"
      height="22"
      viewBox="0 0 24 24"
      fill="none"
      stroke="var(--color-ffb347)"
      stroke-width="2"
      stroke-linecap="round"
      stroke-linejoin="round"
      ><path d="M12 9v4"></path><path d="M12 17h.01"></path><path
        d="M10.3 3.9 1.8 18a2 2 0 0 0 1.7 3h17a2 2 0 0 0 1.7-3L13.7 3.9a2 2 0 0 0-3.4 0z"
      ></path></svg
    ><p>
      Menus, commands and prices move around every few months. Where this guide
      gives steps, treat them as the shape of the thing and check the live docs
      for the exact wording: <a href="https://docs.claude.com"
        >docs.claude.com</a
      >, <a href="https://delta.dev/docs">delta.dev/docs</a> and <a
        href="https://zed.dev/docs">zed.dev/docs</a
      >.
    </p></Callout
  >
</div>
